# Vulnerability details

## Impact
At line 549 within the `_swap()` function's code, the internal call to the function `_checkBalances()` checks the reserve for token `x` is carried out using `xi + specifiedAmount` instead of `xi + roundedSpecifiedAmount`. When using `roundedSpecifiedAmount` passes the check balance, but using `specifiedAmount` may cause the check balance to revert.

## Proof of Concept
The function `_checkBalances()` is employed to verify whether the token reserve in the pool satisfies specific conditions. The reserves of tokens y and x should exceed MIN_BALANCE, and their ratio should also fall within the MIN_M and MAX_M range.
```solidity=809
    function _checkBalances(int256 x, int256 y) private pure {
        if (x < MIN_BALANCE || y < MIN_BALANCE) revert BalanceError(x,y);
        int128 finalBalanceRatio = y.divi(x);
        if (finalBalanceRatio < MIN_M) revert BoundaryError(x,y);
        else if (MAX_M <= finalBalanceRatio) revert BoundaryError(x,y);
    }
```


The variable `specifiedAmount` represents the reserve of tokens the user intends to swap in or out. 

The variable `roundedSpecifiedAmount` represents the quantity of tokens after accounting for fees.

Consequently, this implies that the data from the deduction is not being utilized. As a result, the accuracy of the validation process within the `_checkBalances()` function might be somewhat compromised.

